--TEST--
The empty subject is a fixed point of every `Core\Str` member that answers a string, and `lines` is the one that has to decide what an empty subject holds — spec § 1, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *agreement* shape over the one boundary every transformation in § 1 is
// written around. Each member is asked the empty subject and what is counted
// is how many gave it back, not what each answered — a member that grew its
// own idea of what an empty subject means (a space, a marker, a one-entry
// answer) fails here while still reading correctly on its own line.
//
// `lines` is held out below rather than counted here: it answers
// `array<string>`, so "gave the subject back" is not a question that can be
// asked of it, and the empty subject is exactly where counting lines has to
// part from PHP's `explode`, which answers a list holding one empty string.

string $empty = "";
int $fixed = 0;
int $asked = 0;

$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::lowerFirst($empty) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// `rule:types/string-is-utf8`'s grapheme walk over no graphemes.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::reverse($empty) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// The trim family's boundary: nothing to strip is not the same question as
// everything stripped, and both answer the empty subject.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::trimStart($empty) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// UAX #15 over the empty sequence — every normal form of nothing is nothing.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::normalize($empty, Core\NormalForm::Nfc) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// A pair table with nowhere to match it.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::replaceAll($empty, ["a" => "b"]) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// The one member here that is *given* an offset, so the empty subject is also
// the only offset it admits.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::replaceRange($empty, 0, null, $empty) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

// A width nothing can exceed.
$asked = $asked + 1;
if (Core\Str::wrap($empty, 8) == $empty) { $fixed = $fixed + 1; }

echo "fixed=", $fixed as string, "/", $asked as string, "\n";

// `lines` at the same boundary and one step either side of it. The trailing
// terminator is the row that discriminates: a subject ending in a newline has
// as many lines as one that does not, because a terminator ends a line rather
// than starting another.
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Str::lines($empty)), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Str::lines("a")), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Str::lines("a\n")), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Str::lines("a\n\nb")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
fixed=7/7
[]
["a"]
["a"]
["a","","b"]
